            <div xml:id="d552">

                <pb facs="0916" n="780" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg780"/>

                <head>No. 552.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Sir L. S. Sackville
                        West.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-02-07"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 7,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    to-day of your note dated the 5th instant, in which, having reference to the
                    case of certain American fishing vessels in distress at Halifax, you inform me
                    that the Marquis of Salisbury has telegraphed to you to the effect that
                    permission had been granted to land part of their perishable cargo in order to
                    operate <hi rendition="#italic">bona fide</hi> repairs, but without prejudice to
                    the question of right.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
